From 555cb48abd6edeca413305bf3c8b01fc69547ca8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <cruz@tbu.net>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 09:48:20 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 045/206] feat(shorebird): --print_dd_function_identity_to,
 row-set exact on six fixtures

Ninth of their eleven private flags. Our flag surface is now a strict subset of
theirs: only print_dd_resolution_to and print_shorebird_info remain, both
genuinely blocked on the DD IL subsystem and the three-stage mapper.

The row predicate was established per entry, not by arithmetic on counts. The
previous reading -- one row per global object pool entry, keyed by pool index --
does not survive contact with the data: the raw pool has 1421 entries (1323 obj
+ 92 native + 6 unknown) and no subset of it is 1380. The 1380 index space is
analyze_snapshot --shorebird's `functions` list, i.e. the serializer's code
list, which is why their read point sits in Serializer::PrepareInstructions
where code_index_ is built. Over their base.ddid, with zero exceptions either
way:

  IsAllocationStubCode() -> row, kind 255                             (87)
  IsTypeTestStubCode()   -> no row  ([Stub] Type Test Type:)         (110)
  IsStubCode()           -> no row  ([unknown stub])                   (4)
  owner is a Function    -> row, kind = Function::kind()            (1179)

library_kernel_offset carries Library::kernel_library_index(), not a byte
offset -- Library has no kernel_offset at all, so the name is theirs and the
value is forced. Alloc-stub keys zero kernel_offset and signature_hash, matching
all 87 of their rows.

Verified against their own gen_snapshot output on six fixtures: row multisets
equal and file sizes equal (14925/14874/15242 both sides). 73/73 shorebird tests
pass. Two negative controls: perturbing library_kernel_offset by one moves
exactly the 1179 function rows and leaves the 87 stub rows alone; giving
ForAllocStub the natural wrong kernel_offset turns the new test red (255).

KNOWN GAP: emission order is not reproduced, so the files are not byte
identical. Theirs is one descending run of 1187 rows (1379 -> 29) followed by 79
rows in ~52 short runs; those tail rows pair up with a constant difference of
797 (825/28, 824/27, ..., 797/0), which looks like two interleaved descending
streams. We emit ascending, and sorted(theirs) == ours exactly.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

